okay so the topic of today's video will  be cellular immunity versus humoral  immunity so let's go ahead and get  started so as we get started I first  want to focus on cellular immunity now  one of the main points the major points  of focus is that during cellular  immunity T cells are going to be created  that will ultimately destroy infected  cells of your own body  well how does this process of cellular  immunity work more number one a person  needs to be exposed to a pathogen and so  here are some viruses that have just  entered your body pretend notice on the  outside are some lumps that are called  antigens and these are proteins that are  found on the outside of cells and  viruses and all cells and viruses have  them they're lumps of protein and they  help to identify and mark cells that  belong to us versus cells that are  foreign and so what happens is a virus  or a bacteria in this case in my  animation it'll be a virus a virus is  infecting this particular body cell and  now that the cells infected as you might  know viruses will hijack and take over  the cell and turn the cell into a virus  making Factory and as it does that as  the cell is being basically tricked into  making new viruses what happens is some  proteins of the pathogen of the virus of  the bacteria will actually be collected  and displayed on the outside of the cell  membrane of this protective particular  infected cell of your body here that's  gonna be important important step later  on when we get to you know destroying  this particular pathogen so now you  notice that there are other pathogens  that are inside this person's body well  along comes a white blood cell phagocyte  which will devour and swallow up and  engulf this pathogen through the act of  phagocytosis and so here our Fager site  has just engulfed and swallowed the  pathogen now what happens next I think  is kind of interesting  you know the analogy I like to make kind  of takes us back to medieval times and  in medieval times prisoners may have  been executed and their bodies may have  been cut into pieces and body parts  would be then displayed at various parts  of the kingdom as a reminder of what  happens to those who try to lead a  rebellion against the king now that's a  little analogy for what happens next so  what happens next is our Faga site will  destroy the pathogen but then present on  the surface of itself it'll present  antigens of the now destroyed pathogen  so this is now known as a antigen  displaying phagocyte so now that the  phagocyte is displaying antigens what  happens is a t-cell a type of white  blood cell called a t-cell an  inactivated t-cell will come along and  bind to the antigen displaying Faga site  now t-cells don't actually look like the  letter T I've kind of just drawn it that  way to help help us kind of keep track  of what everything is  but this inactivated T cell is gonna be  really important in getting rid of the  pathogen that's you know getting a sick  so now that the T cell has binded to the  antigen display in phagocyte the antigen  display and Faga site will release  proteins that will be picked up by this  in activated T cells and the proteins  will actually activate the T cell the T  cell once activated will be stimulated  to multiply and divide and replicate  into a couple categories one category  being memory T cells  so as my activated T cell now begins a  multiply notice how its multiplying into  memory T cells now these memory T cells  are really important in fighting the  pathogen should you encounter it later  on at some other time in your life you  will have these memory cells memory T  cells built up in reserve and they'll  spring into action should you ever  encounter this pathogen again but let's  get rid of the memory T cells and let's  just focus on  carrying this current infection so the  other category that's going to be  created now that the memory T t-cells  have been created are going to be the  active T cells so not only will the T  cell divide into memory T cells but that  T cell will divide into active or plasma  T cells so again the antigen presenting  Vega site has released proteins that  will stimulate the T cell to multiply  and now we have basically an army of  these T cells to not only fight the  pathogen should it encounter later on in  your life with the memory T cells but  also it now has created an army to fight  the current infection and that's really  what the active T cells are the plasma T  cells do they fight the current  infection so what they're gonna do is  they're gonna one of them is gonna bind  to this infected body cell remember the  infected body cell is displaying  antigens of the virus as well and so the  activated t cell will recognize the  pathogens antigens as being foreign into  the t cell well attached to the infected  body cell and the t cell the active T  cell will begin to release proteins and  enzymes that will ultimately destroy  this infected body cells this type of T  cell by the way is called a cytotoxic T  cell it kills this particular cell of  your body and over time notice how the  cell is destroyed and then healthy  neighboring cells will multiply through  mitosis and kind of reoccupy that space  okay so that was cellular immunity now I  want to shift focus to humoral immunity  now the main major point I want you to  know with humoral immunity is that  during humoral immunity b-cells are  created that are gonna produce a whole  bunch of antibodies with the purpose of  fighting off whatever the pathogen is so  first of all the person is going to be  exposed to a path  the gin and here are some pathogens that  have just entered this person's perhaps  their bloodstream and next what happens  is b-cells will already have membrane  bound antibodies in them so here comes a  b-cell and again no it does not look  like the letter B I've kind of just done  that so we can kind of keep track what  everything is  but notice how there are these Y shaped  structures poking out of the B cell  these are the membrane bound antibodies  and the B cell has these membrane bound  antibodies that will attach itself to  the matching antigen of the virus or the  bacteria or the pathogen whatever is  foreign and what happens next is the B  cell will engulf and swallow up and  destroy and break down the pathogen  through doing the act of phagocytosis  and just like we saw a moment ago this  kind of reminds me of medieval times  where again the body parts of victims  are are displayed around the kingdom as  a reminder not to rebel against the king  so cells of the immune system kind of go  medieval and break down and display the  body parts of the pathogen and so the  antigens of the pathogen are going to be  displayed on the surface of this  particular B cell right here now this is  now called an antigen presenting B cell  and so what happens next is a t-cell  known as a helper T a helper T cell or  bind to this antigen presenting B cell  now there's a reason it's called a  helper T cell it's going to help  activate this particular B cell and it  does so by releasing various proteins so  the helper T cell will release these  proteins which will activate this  antigen presenting B cell and now that  the antigen presenting B cell has been  activated the B cell will be stimulated  to multiply and divide  to basically create an army of itself  and when it multiplies and divides it'll  mult multiply and divide into a couple  categories of b-cells number one being  the memory b-cells and as we said a  moment ago the memory b-cells are  created and are used in case you ever  encounter the pathogen at a future time  in your life you will have this immune  system army of b-cells already built up  in reserve to fight the pathogen so  let's remove the b-cells and let's focus  on our current infection and so as the  b-cell has now been activated and  multiplies it will also produce active  b-cells the army of b-cells that will  fight this current infection and so what  happens is the b-cells are basically  turned into a factory to make antibodies  and notice how inside of the b-cell are  these Y shaped structures these are the  antibodies the antibodies are produced  and released into the blood plasma into  the bloodstream and so now that your  blood has antibodies flowing through it  well what happens next well if we just  focus on the antibodies that have been  released from the b-cells the antibodies  are now flowing through the blood well  how do the antibodies actually destroy  the pathogen well there's a variety of  ways one what they do is they will  simply attach themselves to the antigens  of the pathogen and by doing so it just  interferes with the pathogens ability to  infect other cells  another way that antibodies are helpful  antibodies will actually kind of like a  little handcuff attach itself to an  antigen of a pathogen and then kind of  draw and cause a couple of them to clump  together like little handcuffs and as  this happens there are other antibodies  will attach themselves to the pathogens  to this big clump of pathogens and this  really tags the pathogen as being a  foreign invader and now that the  of viruses or bacteria now that the  clump of pathogens has been tagged along  comes a Vegas ID a white blood cell  which will swallow up the the big mass  of viruses and bacteria and then are  destroyed by by the phagocyte after it  swallows up and and is breaks them down  and so as time passes the current  infection is is defeated by the cells of  your immune system and so there you have  it there is I hope a nice side-by-side  comparison of humoral vs. cellular  immunity so if you're in my biology  class you know try this practice quiz  pause the video answer them on a  separate sheet of paper and bring me  your answers I'd love to check them for  accuracy so I hope you found this video  helpful thanks for watching
 

